"""Definition-driven family of the lifecycle base, as the skeleton ships it; internal.

``_lifecycle`` owns the sequence, the settlement of a lost reply and the
result and failure contracts; this module supplies the steps from a curated
definition. The rendered ``_family`` binding supplies the product client,
error schema and compatibility check.

Client contract (exercised by tests/unit/plugins/modules/test_contract.py):
``list(definition)`` returns a complete listing or raises; ``lookup(definition,
keys)`` permits absence only under that definition's documented by-key rule.
``get(definition, identifier)`` reads one object, never infers absence from a
write response. ``create``, ``update`` and ``delete`` perform at most one write;
update preserves unmanaged fields. Their responses remain private. ``encode``
and ``decode`` translate individual field values, not lifecycle decisions.
``probe`` is read-only. ``version`` discovers once per client and caches.

Clients classify failures by status before parsing any body. Listing rows,
lookup records and mutation responses can contain secrets: only project()
publishes reviewed fields. After a possible mutation, a failure remains unknown
until independent read-back establishes the outcome. No blind write retry.

A definition's size bounds apply to input values only; a stored object of any
size is read, compared, deleted and reported as is.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import re
from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import Any, Callable, NoReturn

from ansible_collections.foundata.utils.plugins.module_utils import errors

